th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","history_only":true,"curation_sha256":"402117cd2ecc28eaf6387aaefcadec5f0c0540f0912f8a96983845daf37ec8e2","concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/taylors-theorem.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/taylors-theorem.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylors_theorem_03645578_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In Taylor's theorem, what is the Peano form of the remainder?","options":["$R_k(x) = h_k(x)+(x-a)^k$ with $h_k(x) = 1$","$R_k(x) = h_k(x)(x-a)^k$ with $\\lim_{x\\to a}h_k(x) = 0$","$R_k(x) = \\dfrac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}$","$R_k(x) = 0$ for every $k$-times differentiable function"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"aa430be6b3dd23ccb34116c5","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylors_theorem_03645578_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $k = 2$ and centre $a = 0$, which polynomial part appears before the Peano remainder?","options":["$f(0)+f'(0)x+\\dfrac{f''(0)}{2!}x^2$","$f(0)+f'(0)x+f''(0)x^2$","$f(x)+f'(x)x+\\dfrac{f''(x)}{2!}x^2$","$f(0)x+f'(0)x^2+\\dfrac{f''(0)}{2!}x^3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"633fb2935bc8c1a875fad4fb","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylors_theorem_03645578_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What assumption on $f$ is stated in Taylor's theorem?","options":["$f\\colon\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ is $k$-times differentiable at $x = a$.","$f$ must be real analytic on all of $\\mathbb{R}$.","$f$ must itself be a polynomial of degree at most $k$.","$f$ must have no derivatives at $a$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ada408bf93d42d7cef40e55f","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylors_theorem_03645578_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $R_3(x) = h_3(x)(x-a)^3$ and $\\lim_{x\\to a}h_3(x) = 0$, which little-$o$ form follows?","options":["$R_3(x) = o((x-a)^2)$ is the only conclusion","$R_3(x) = o((x-a)^3)$","$R_3(x) = 3!$","$R_3(x) = f^{(3)}(a)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0e144c1252fa303516943061","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylors_theorem_03645578_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does Taylor's theorem's statement 'not necessarily real analytic' matter?","options":["Because differentiability and real analyticity mean exactly the same thing.","Because real analytic functions cannot have Taylor polynomials.","Because the theorem applies only when no derivatives exist.","Because the finite Peano form needs no full-series equality."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3adc274078e80145feebc7f2","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylors_theorem_03645578_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For an integer $k\\ge1$, which condition is equivalent to $R_k(x)=o((x-a)^k)$ as $x\\to a$?","options":["The error divided by $(x-a)^k$ stays bounded as $x\\to a$.","The error tends to $0$ as $x\\to a$, with no rate attached.","The error divided by $(x-a)^k$ tends to $0$ as $x\\to\\infty$.","The error divided by $(x-a)^k$ tends to $0$ as $x\\to a$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"dbe7ed511195604a5cee9d82","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylors_theorem_03645578_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A proposed first-order Taylor expansion is $f(x) = f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)+h_1(x)$ with $h_1(x)\\to 0$. What is missing for the Peano form?","options":["The remainder should be $h_1(x)(x-a)$, not just $h_1(x)$.","The term $f(a)$ should be removed.","The derivative $f'(a)$ should be replaced by $f(a)$.","The expansion should contain only a second derivative term."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c95ec16cbd867bacb789f227","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylors_theorem_03645578_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does $R_k(x)=o((x-a)^k)$ mean?","options":["The remainder is a constant multiple of $(x-a)^k$ as $x\\to a$.","The remainder is smaller than $(x-a)^k$ in the little-$o$ sense as $x\\to a$.","The remainder is at most a constant times $(x-a)^{k+1}$ as $x\\to a$.","The remainder is smaller than $|x-a|^k$ in absolute value for every $x$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"525c80289dce0dd3778d2340","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylors_theorem_03645578_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $k=1$, which polynomial part appears in Taylor's theorem at $a$?","options":["$f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)$","$f(a)+f''(a)(x-a)^2$","$f'(a)$","$f(a)(x-a)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2934759ab3383b01e8f1b0fd","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylors_theorem_03645578_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For the remainder $R_2(x)=(x-a)^3$, what is $\\lim_{x\\to a}R_2(x)/(x-a)^2$?","options":["$1$","$+\\infty$","$-1$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0856a8a15056ee38ccbde01e","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylors_theorem_03645578_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"In the expansion of $\\mathrm{e}^x$ at $0$ to order $2$, which polynomial part is used?","options":["$1+x+\\frac{x^2}{2}$","$1+x+x^2$","$x+\\frac{x^2}{2}$","$1+x+\\frac{x^2}{2}+\\frac{x^3}{6}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"332d114134aff89b63206f5f","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylors_theorem_03645578_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does Taylor's theorem say about the approximation error near $a$?","options":["It is exactly zero once $x$ is close enough to $a$.","It is $(x-a)^k$ times a factor that tends to $0$.","It is $(x-a)^k$ times a factor that tends to $1$.","It is $(x-a)^{k+1}$ times a constant factor."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8f4927800522eb1239cf6573","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_ta